    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Swelling of the face and legs with cola-colored urine, less urine, raised blood pressure, and reduced kidney function (acute nephritic picture), two to three weeks after a febrile sore throat and followed by aching knees and ankles, crampy abdominal pain, and vomiting; cause not yet confirmed at intake; urgent nephrology consultation requested</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R60.1</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem anchored to his documented febrile illness of 1784; superseded by the diagnosis at the nephrology consultation.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Group A streptococcal pharyngitis, rapid antigen test positive at intake with a mildly red throat two to three weeks after the sore throat began; penicillin V started</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>J02.0</td><td>S — Synthetic modern infection that triggers the episode; his recurrent sore throats are a retrospective interpretation (CLM-0015).</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis: acute nephritic syndrome with red cell casts, hypertension, edema, and a rise in creatinine, low C3 with normal C4, and raised ASO and anti-DNase B after group A streptococcal pharyngitis; no kidney biopsy, because the course is typical; acute rheumatic fever excluded by the Jones criteria and a normal echocardiogram; synthetic modern episode linked to his documented illness of 1784</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>N00.9</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis made at the nephrology consultation; it makes no claim about his real illnesses or the cause of his death.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

