  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (N00.9)</content>. The presenting nephritic picture now has its cause. Two to three weeks after a group A streptococcal sore throat he has edema, hypertension, red cells at 45 per high-power field with red cell casts, a urine protein-to-creatinine ratio of 1100 mg/g, and creatinine 1.78 mg/dL (eGFR 53), from 1.62 two days ago. C3 at intake was low at 42 mg/dL with a normal C4 of 24, and ASO 620 and anti-DNase B 980 IU/mL confirm the recent infection. Dr. Okonkwo-Brandt agrees that this is typical post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis and that a biopsy is not needed now. Potassium 5.0 mmol/L. No signs of hypertensive emergency or pulmonary edema, so outpatient care continues. [S]<list><item>Continue furosemide 40 mg each morning until he is back to his usual weight.</item><item>Sodium below 2 g a day and about 1.5 liters of fluid a day while swollen; daily weights.</item><item>Creatinine and potassium in about a week, then a full review in two weeks with C3.</item><item>Add amlodipine if blood pressure stays at 140/90 or higher once the swelling has gone.</item><item>No NSAIDs.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Group A streptococcal pharyngitis (J02.0)</content>. Throat settling on day three of penicillin V. [S]<list><item>Finish the ten-day course.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Joint aches after the streptococcal infection: acute rheumatic fever considered</content>. Polyarthralgia counts as one minor Jones criterion. His low fever at home and 37.6 °C at intake are below the 38.5 °C threshold, and ESR 38 mm/h and CRP 18 mg/L are below the minor-criterion thresholds. There is no arthritis on examination, no chorea, rash, or nodules, the PR interval is 156 ms, and the echocardiogram today shows normal valves without pathological regurgitation (LVEF 62%), so there is no clinical or subclinical carditis. With no major criterion, acute rheumatic fever is not diagnosed, and secondary prophylaxis is not needed. [S]<list><item>Return for swollen, hot joints, chest pain, or breathlessness.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Standardized office blood pressure</td><td>Every visit; treat if 140/90 or higher while nephritic, or with symptoms</td><td>Completed today</td><td>148/94 mmHg on furosemide, from 162/100</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Volume status: weight, edema, lungs, jugular venous pressure</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Weight 63.0 kg, down 1.2 kg; 1+ ankle edema; mild periorbital edema; lungs clear; jugular venous pressure 3 cm</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Creatinine with eGFR, BUN, and potassium</td><td>At diagnosis, about weekly while creatinine is raised or on a diuretic, then at six to eight weeks</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Creatinine 1.78 mg/dL (eGFR 53), BUN 34 mg/dL, potassium 5.0 mmol/L; creatinine still rising, so a repeat is due in about a week</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Urine microscopy for red cells and casts, and urine protein-to-creatinine ratio</td><td>At diagnosis, at six weeks, at six months, and at a year, until hematuria and proteinuria clear</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Red cells 45 per high-power field with red cell casts; protein-to-creatinine ratio 1100 mg/g</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Complement C3 (with C4 at diagnosis)</td><td>At diagnosis, at about two weeks, and at six to eight weeks until normal</td><td>Up to date</td><td>C3 42 mg/dL (low) and C4 24 mg/dL (normal) at intake; repeat at two weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Evidence of recent streptococcal infection (rapid antigen test or culture, ASO, anti-DNase B)</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Rapid antigen test positive, ASO 620 IU/mL, and anti-DNase B 980 IU/mL at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Serology for other glomerular diseases (ANA, ANCA, anti-GBM) and kidney biopsy</td><td>Only if atypical: C3 low beyond eight weeks, creatinine still rising, nephrotic-range proteinuria persisting, or systemic features</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>The course is typical; Dr. Okonkwo-Brandt will biopsy only if it becomes atypical</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>ECG for PR prolongation (Jones minor criterion)</td><td>When joint symptoms or fever follow a streptococcal infection</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Sinus rhythm; PR interval 156 ms, normal</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Echocardiogram with Doppler for clinical or subclinical carditis</td><td>When joint symptoms or fever follow a streptococcal infection</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Normal valves; no pathological mitral or aortic regurgitation; LVEF 62%</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Jones criteria review (major and minor manifestations with evidence of streptococcal infection)</td><td>When joint symptoms or fever follow a streptococcal infection</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No major criterion; one minor criterion (polyarthralgia); fever, ESR 38 mm/h and CRP 18 mg/L below the minor thresholds; not met</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Loop diuretic safety: potassium, creatinine, dizziness, and when to stop</td><td>Within a week of starting and at each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Potassium 5.0 mmol/L; no dizziness on standing</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medication reconciliation: NSAID avoidance and dose review</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Ibuprofen stopped at intake; acetaminophen only</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antibiotic course for group A streptococcus: completion and allergy</td><td>At start and at completion</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Day three of penicillin V; no rash or allergy</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Sodium below 2 g a day, fluid limit while edematous, and daily weights</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Keeping salt low and fluids near 1.5 liters; weighing each morning</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Testing of household contacts without symptoms</td><td>Not routine</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Household contacts without symptoms are not tested; anyone with a sore throat should be seen</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Nephrology consultation</td><td>For an acute nephritic syndrome with a rising creatinine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Okonkwo-Brandt today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Swelling gone and back to his usual weight of about 61 kg within two weeks. In progress; target 2026-09-29 [S]</item><item>Blood pressure below 130/80 mmHg with no blood pressure medicine needed by six weeks. In progress; target 2026-10-27 [S]</item><item>Creatinine back to normal by six weeks. In progress; target 2026-10-27 [S]</item><item>C3 back to normal within eight weeks. In progress; target 2026-10-27 [S]</item><item>Urine protein-to-creatinine ratio below 150 mg/g. In progress; target 2026-10-27 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: KDIGO 2021 Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Glomerular Diseases (Kidney Int 2021;100(4S):S1-S276) (2021); Revision of the Jones Criteria for the Diagnosis of Acute Rheumatic Fever in the Era of Doppler Echocardiography: a scientific statement (Gewitz MH, et al. Circulation 2015;131(20):1806-18) (2015); Clinical Practice Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Group A Streptococcal Pharyngitis: 2012 Update (Shulman ST, et al. Clin Infect Dis 2012;55(10):e86-102) (2012); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, chronic kidney disease and hypertension monographs (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Creatinine and potassium in about a week. Review in two weeks with kidney labs and C3. Call for a weight gain of 1 kg in a day, less urine, a severe headache, vision change, or breathlessness. Next scheduled visit 2026-09-29: two-week review: fluid, blood pressure, kidney function and C3; stop the diuretic. [S]</paragraph></text>

